[{"result":{"data":{"json":{"id":"155","stub":{"id":"155","section":"1) Bridge on Forest Service Route 7 to Trimmer Springs Road","altname":"","river":"Big Creek (Kings River Trib)","difficulty":"IIItoV","states":["USA-CAL"]},"primaryGaugeStatus":null,"pointOfInterests":[{"type":"put-in","description":null,"id":"111387","location":{"latitude":"36.9662","longitude":"-119.232"},"name":"Put In","distance":"0","photoID":null,"difficulty":"N/A","approximate":false},{"type":"access","description":"The old gage dam is obvious as you drive past.   The location marked on the river map is a very rough guess.   The boating looks significantly harder upstream in my recolection so it used to be a common launching point.","id":"100635","location":{"latitude":"36.943199157714844","longitude":"-119.23899841308594"},"name":"Gage dam","distance":"2","photoID":"0","difficulty":"N/A","approximate":true},{"type":"access","description":"Below the bridge, drop down some very big but junky rapids..  Take out on the obvious slabs then hike uphill to the road (usually on river right.)","id":"100636","location":{"latitude":"36.90811538696289","longitude":"-119.2435531616211"},"name":"Take out: below Bridge","distance":"5","photoID":"868511","difficulty":"N/A","approximate":true},{"type":"takeout","description":null,"id":"116656","location":{"latitude":"36.892","longitude":"-119.246"},"name":"Take Out","distance":"6","photoID":null,"difficulty":"N/A","approximate":false}],"alerts":[],"photos":[{"id":"868507","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"868506","description":"These rapids leading to the bridge are really fun with water.","caption":"Upstream of Trimmer bridge","poi_name":"","subject":"","author":"Paul Martzen","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/868507","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/868507"}},{"id":"868508","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"868506","description":"These rapids leading to the bridge are really fun with water.  Watch out for brush and trees though.","caption":"Upstream of Trimmer bridge -2","poi_name":"","subject":"","author":"Paul Martzen","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/868508","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/868508"}},{"id":"868509","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"868506","description":"Looking at the rapid below the bridge and debating the lines.   The hillsides sure are green in February","caption":"Debating routes from the Trimmer bridge","poi_name":"","subject":"","author":"Paul Martzen","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/868509","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/868509"}},{"id":"868510","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"868506","description":"The rapid below the bridge is one of the biggest and also junkiest of the whole run.   Always seemed pretty fun to crash down it though.  There is probably vertical pin potential, so scout it out.   The steepest and junkiest part is in the middle of this picture, mostly hidden behind the trees.  \r\n\r\nThe obvious slabs on river right are a good take out, but you can keep paddling on down towards the lake also.   There is a pretty good trail up to the road, where the road turns to the west.  ","caption":"Rapids below the Trimmer bridge","poi_name":"","subject":"","author":"Paul Martzen","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/868510","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/868510"}},{"id":"879356","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"879879","description":"This is one of a number of pretty nice bedrock rapids on the run.  They alternate with more brushy and rocky rapids.  ","caption":"Bedrock Rapids","poi_name":"","subject":"","author":"Paul Martzen","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/879356","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/879356"}},{"id":"879357","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"879879","description":"This strange structure is on a side ravine along Big Creek.  It makes for a fascinating short side hike.  A number of ravines in this area have similar structures.  I do not know their original purpose or when they were built.   As soon as you leave Trimmer Springs Road to drive up Big Creek Road, start looking up every ravine to see what is there.","caption":"Cisturn","poi_name":"","subject":"","author":"Paul Martzen","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/879357","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/879357"}},{"id":"879358","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"879879","description":"Another strange structure on a different side ravine along Big Creek.  It makes for a fascinating short side hike.  A number of ravines in this area have similar structures.  I do not know their original purpose or when they were built.   As soon as you leave Trimmer Springs Road to drive up Big Creek Road, start looking up every ravine to see what is there.","caption":"Cisturn 2","poi_name":"","subject":"","author":"Paul Martzen","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/879358","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/879358"}},{"id":"868511","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"868506","description":"This is a good view of the slabs take-out.   The drops visible in this photo are clean and fun.    \r\n\r\nThe rapid below the bridge is one of the biggest and also junkiest of the whole run.   Always seemed pretty fun to crash down it though.   The junky stuff is out of view to the left of this picture.  \r\n\r\nYou can keep paddling on down towards the lake past this point.  I never have, but Richard Penney thought it was pretty decent paddling if there is good flow.   There is a pretty good trail up to the road, where the road turns to the west.  ","caption":"Rapids below the Trimmer bridge -2","poi_name":"","subject":"","author":"Paul Martzen","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/868511","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/868511"}}],"detail":{"id":"155","permitUrl":null,"permitInfo":null,"description":"Big Creek is a small tributary of the Kings River. As one travels around Pine Flat Reservoir on the way to the Kings River, the road crosses bridges over two major inlets after passing Trimmer Springs. The first inlet and bridge is [Sycamore Creek](http://www.americanwhitewater.org/rivers/id/309/). The second bridge and inlet is Big Creek.\n\nBig Creek is small and brushy, runnable during and after decent winter rain storms. It is fairly technical but not super steep. The streambed alternates between nice bedrock drops and rocky rapids. At low and moderate flows this creek is suitable for kayakers learning technical paddling and how to boat through bushes. At high flows this creek is dangerous because of the many bushes and trees in the streambed, acting as strainers. (When this creek is too high, nearby [Sycamore Creek](http://www.americanwhitewater.org/rivers/id/309/) will often be at a good level.)\n\n**Put in:**  A dirt road parrallels the creek, so paddlers can put in or take out at almost any point. The most common put-in is at the remains of an old gauge and dam. But sometimes people drive all the way to where a bridge crosses the creek (elevation 1480 feet) and the road leaves the creek.  The biggest drops are below the bridge but seems like the brush was worse up there as well.   Worth a look since it is roadside.\n\n**Take out:**  The common take-outs are just upstream of the Trimmer Springs Rd. bridge, or just a short ways below the bridge on river right after a big rapid (elevation 930 feet). You can also continue boating down into the lake bottom, with the penalty of hiking farther uphill to the road afterwards.   There is a trail leading down from the road on river right near the confluence with the Kings river, just where the road turns to the west.\n\nIn the summer time, Big Creek has a variety of nice swimming holes suitable for kids and families.\n\nWhile at Big Creek, there are some strange old structures to explore. Just before you get to the Big Creek bridge (approaching from Trimmer) look to your left into the last two ravines before the bridge. Continue onto Big Creek road and look at the next 3 or 4 ravines as well. In each of these small gulleys, stairs lead up to large concrete basins with odd flumes and gates. They are fascinating to climb around on, but I have no definite idea what they were constructed for or when. One suggestion is that they were contstructed to collect water for use in a mining operation, or perhaps they were used in the mineral seperation process.   A recent (Feb 2010) conversation with a retired Forest Service lands officer suggested that these structures were built in the 1920's for a rain runoff study.  She was not positive, however.","averageGradient":"110","maxGradient":"0","length":"6.4","correlations":[],"pointOfInterestIDs":["111387","100635","100636","116656"],"geometry":{"type":"LineString","coordinates":[[-119.2319909296717,36.96612021998373],[-119.23230239203457,36.96608480928796],[-119.23230792536793,36.96607720928796],[-119.23237679203447,36.966019875954714],[-119.23246039203435,36.96595127595481],[-119.23248352536761,36.96593600928816],[-119.23248872536766,36.96587874262161],[-119.23249119203427,36.965852075954956],[-119.23246059203431,36.96580247595506],[-119.23224692536802,36.96553547595545],[-119.2321933920348,36.9654630092889],[-119.23216299203484,36.96542860928895],[-119.23215665870147,36.965421875955656],[-119.23210212536821,36.965363809